 # Hardware-Accelerated Volume Rendering
 
-Also called: Texture Mapping Hardware Assisted Rendering
+Also called: GPU-Accelerated Volume Rendering, Texture Mapping Hardware Assisted Rendering
 
 ## Overview
-Hardware-Accelerated Volume Rendering is a direct volume ray casting technique that offloads the computationally intensive sampling and compositing steps to the GPU's texture hardware and fragment shaders [S1, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of software-based ray casting by utilizing 3D texture memory for trilinear interpolation and programmable shaders for front-to-back compositing [5616-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md]. It differs from [Shear-Warp](shear-warp.md) by avoiding the final warp step and performing classification after resampling, and from [Custom Hardware Units](8284-custom-hardware-units.md) by relying on general-purpose programmable GPUs rather than dedicated ASICs [9529-3d-texture-mapping.md][8284-custom-hardware-units.md].
+Hardware-Accelerated Volume Rendering is a family of techniques that exploit Graphics Processors (GPUs) to achieve interactive rates for structured grids fitting in main memory [S1]. It solves the performance bottleneck of software-based ray casting by utilizing hardware parallel logic units, cache coherency rearrangement, and native hardware support for trilinear interpolation and alpha compositing [S1]. It differs from [Shear-Warp](shear-warp.md) by avoiding the final warp step and performing classification after resampling, and from [Custom Hardware Units](8284-custom-hardware-units.md) by relying on general-purpose programmable GPUs rather than dedicated ASICs [G].
 
