 ### MCOP GPU Ray-Casting Pipeline
-A GPU-based volume rendering pipeline that uses a Mixed Camera and Object Perspective (MCOP) projection to render light fields for displays with holographic screens, bypassing rasterizer interpolation limitations [S2]. It renders proxy geometry (coarsely tessellated bounding volume) to a fragment shader. The vertex shader computes MCOP projection using equations 1 and 2, passing normalized and unnormalized projected coordinates to the fragment stage. The fragment shader computes ray entry, direction, length, and performs ray-marching compositing [S2]. It differs from standard single-pass GPU ray-casters because it cannot rely on rasterizer interpolation to pass combined 3D and projected data from vertex to fragment shader due to MCOP perspective [S2].
+A GPU-based volume rendering pipeline that uses a Mixed Camera and Object Perspective (MCOP) projection to render light fields for displays with holographic screens, bypassing rasterizer interpolation limitations [S3]. It renders proxy geometry (coarsely tessellated bounding volume) to a fragment shader [S3]. The vertex shader computes MCOP projection using equations 1 and 2, passing normalized and unnormalized projected coordinates to the fragment stage [S3]. The fragment shader computes ray entry, direction, length, and performs ray-marching compositing [S3]. It differs from standard single-pass GPU ray-casters because it cannot rely on rasterizer interpolation to pass combined 3D and projected data from vertex to fragment shader due to MCOP perspective [S3].
 
 ### Generic Render Dispatcher
-A generic render function that switches between backends (currently only 'lut'). It handles type/device checking and delegates to the specific forward method [S3, unofficial]. It checks type and device consistency if `self.strict_types` is true; otherwise, it casts `rays_o` and `rays_d` to `self.dtype` and `self.device`. If `backend == 'lut'`, it calls `self.forward(...)` with the ray data and visualization parameters, raising `ValueError` for unknown backends [S3, unofficial]. The `backend` parameter is a string specifying the rendering backend, currently only 'lut' is supported [S3, unofficial].
+A generic render function that switches between backends (currently only 'lut') [S4, unofficial]. It handles type/device checking and delegates to the specific forward method [S4, unofficial]. It checks type and device consistency if `self.strict_types` is true; otherwise, it casts `rays_o` and `rays_d` to `self.dtype` and `self.device` [S4, unofficial]. If `backend == 'lut'`, it calls `self.forward(...)` with the ray data and visualization parameters, raising `ValueError` for unknown backends [S4, unofficial]. The `backend` parameter is a string specifying the rendering backend, currently only 'lut' is supported [S4, unofficial].
 
 ### CUDAVolumetricRender
-A hardware-accelerated volumetric rendering function provided by CUDALink for CUDA-capable graphics cards, performing real volume rendering [S4, unofficial]. It requires the latest CUDA paclet to be installed [S4, unofficial]. The input data must be imported as slices, converted to a packed integer array with depth 3 (elements 0-255) using `ImageData[..., Automatic]` or `ImageData[..., "Byte"]` [S4, unofficial]. The data dimensions must be repartitioned from $d \times h \times w$ to $h \times w \times d$ without changing data order, using a specific conversion function, before calling `CUDAVolumetricRender` [S4, unofficial]. It is a 'real deal' volumetric renderer with a transfer function, unlike the polygon-based method which mimics the effect [S4, unofficial]. Pitfalls include the specific repartitioning requirement (not a transpose) and potential memory allocation errors [S4, unofficial].
