 ## Background
-Hardware-assisted volume rendering emerged as a paradigm to overcome the computational limits of software-based ray casting. Early surveys identified texture mapping hardware-assisted rendering and custom hardware as distinct categories from software-based techniques [S1, unofficial]. The approach leverages the parallel processing power of GPUs, specifically their texture sampling units and fragment shaders, to perform the heavy lifting of ray marching and compositing in real-time [S2]. Custom hardware units were developed earlier to address specific bottlenecks in geometry calculations and compositing for regular datasets, using fixed-function logic and DSPs [8284-custom-hardware-units.md]. The integration of programmable shaders allowed for more flexible implementations, such as the VStudio framework, which decouples rendering logic into modular components [6916-vstudio-framework-volume-rendering-pipeline.md]. More recent developments, such as Triton-accelerated raycasting, focus on optimizing memory bandwidth and kernel fusion for high-resolution rendering [3379-triton-accelerated-volume-raycasting.md].
